WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.708
NARRATOR: They created
a ship of dreams.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:12.912
Phil Gowan: It had to be the
ultimate climax of their lives.

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:15.948
NARRATOR: But for nine
shipbuilders aboard the Titanic,

00:00:15.981 --> 00:00:19.085
the dream became a nightmare.

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.788
And the nightmare...became
a commodity.

00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:28.027
One hundred years later, a
global market for all things

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.331
Titanic leaves the
ship itself exposed.

00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:36.202
Bob Ballard: The Titanic is
really a deep sea museum with

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:38.504
the doors wide open.

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.206
Fred Parkes: That's
my uncle's graveyard,

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.942
and you don't desecrate a grave.

00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:45.711
NARRATOR: National Geographic
explorer-in-residence

00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:49.415
Robert Ballard discovered
the ship 27 years ago.

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:53.753
Now he fears the Titanic
will be lost...again

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:56.189
Bob Ballard: If the
Titanic is not protected,

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:59.258
it'll get stripped until all
the jewels have been taken off

00:00:59.291 --> 00:01:00.393
the old lady's body.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:21.448
NARRATOR: The Titanic is
a dark and silent grave.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:27.420
Here, every object
tells a story...of

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:33.259
triumph...luxury...and loss.

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:38.498
Even the humblest artifacts
fuel a legend that captivates

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.267
millions around the world.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:46.105
Auctioneer: Lot 164, a brown
wisteria chocolate cup in mint

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:48.941
condition, and I'm in
at 350 on this one...

00:01:48.974 --> 00:01:51.210
Nikki: She was just
such an amazing ship.

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:54.280
The thought that within a few
days she just disappeared to

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:56.549
the ocean lost forever...

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:57.984
Engineer: I'm an
engineer by trade,

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.719
so I'm interested
in the mechanical,

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:00.853
in how the ship works.

00:02:00.886 --> 00:02:02.054
Collector: Of the
things I collect,

00:02:02.087 --> 00:02:05.224
I think the things that
interest me pertain to people.

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:08.060
Auctioneer: ...at
75 pounds, 75, 75...

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:09.962
NARRATOR: This is where
collectors put a price tag

00:02:09.995 --> 00:02:13.599
on the global fascination
with the Titanic.

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:15.535
Auctioneer: Eight
hundred pounds!

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:19.338
NARRATOR: Twice a year, Titanic
enthusiasts descend on a small

00:02:19.371 --> 00:02:23.142
town in the English countryside
to visit the auction house of

00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:25.378
Henry Aldridge &amp; Son.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.146
Andrew Aldridge: We are the
world's leaders in the sale

00:02:27.179 --> 00:02:28.581
of Titanic memorabilia.

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:30.750
We have sold hundreds
and hundreds of items.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.352
We hold every world record
relating to the sale

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:35.621
of Titanic memorabilia.

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:38.724
These tiles will cost three
or four hundred pounds.

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:41.661
Here is a remarkable item worth
between two or three thousand

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:42.929
pounds.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.463
Something like this will be
worth between three and five

00:02:44.496 --> 00:02:45.665
thousand pounds.

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.967
This card was posted
from the Titanic.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.437
So, this card is worth
several thousand pounds.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.173
NARRATOR: One of the most
anticipated items in the auction

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:58.911
is a set of keys from a
first class men's lavatory.

00:02:58.944 --> 00:03:00.846
Andrew Aldridge: And what's
significant about these,

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:02.114
they're from the Titanic.

00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:04.484
So they're worth between
30 and 50,000 pounds.

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:12.158
Auction Visitor: Can I have
a look at the large plate?

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:14.260
NARRATOR: It all comes down
to what collectors are willing

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:18.264
to bid to own a piece
of Titanic's history.

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:20.866
Auctioneer: Starting
at 120 pounds.

00:03:20.899 --> 00:03:22.535
Andrew Aldridge: We have phone
bids registered from around

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:23.569
the world.

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:25.671
We got bids from U.S.,
bidders from China,

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:29.141
bidders from Thailand.

00:03:29.174 --> 00:03:31.077
Nikki: I particularly
collect menus.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.812
John: The china is
my main interest.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.680
I've always collected the china.

00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:37.083
Save my pennies and then spend
them all and then save my

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.384
pennies and then spend them all.

00:03:38.417 --> 00:03:41.153
Alan: They've got some
fantastic stuff in the auction.

00:03:41.186 --> 00:03:44.423
One off things, which will
probably never appear again,

00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:49.528
and I suspect items like that
will just go through the roof.

00:03:49.561 --> 00:03:51.530
NARRATOR: As a
reputable auction house,

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:54.533
Aldridge &amp; Son carefully
researches the origins of every

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:57.903
artifact-and never deals
in objects recovered from

00:03:57.936 --> 00:04:00.440
the ocean floor.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.312
But in such a lucrative market,
serious Titanic collectors know

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:08.114
there's a dark side.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.850
Nikki: I think people have
always been interested in

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:13.853
the idea of going down and
retrieving objects from

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.755
the debris field.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:18.257
NARRATOR: And the
threat is real.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.660
John: Someone said something
once about a rivet that someone

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:24.163
offered them for about
10,000 pounds from a wreck.

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:26.432
As long as you got proof
that it is from the Titanic,

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:27.333
you can name your price.

00:04:30.569 --> 00:04:33.372
NARRATOR: A black market in
Titanic artifacts is a nightmare

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:36.475
scenario for Dr. Robert Ballard.

00:04:36.508 --> 00:04:41.380
Bob Ballard: The Titanic is
really a deep sea museum with

00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:42.982
the doors wide open.

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:46.052
There's no lock on the
door, and there's no guard.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:48.754
NARRATOR: Ironically, much
of today's fascination with

00:04:48.787 --> 00:04:52.425
the Titanic stems from Ballard's
discovery of the shipwreck

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:53.860
in 1985.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:10.443
What is that? The boiler!

00:05:14.279 --> 00:05:17.383
Bob Ballard: The boiler for me
was the signature image that

00:05:17.416 --> 00:05:20.119
told us we'd found the Titanic.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:27.460
Go rouse everybody out
of the racks. Wake the ship.

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:29.762
Bob Ballard: There were
two people inside of me

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:30.996
at that moment.

00:05:31.029 --> 00:05:35.501
The professional, who was
elated and happy and excited.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.803
But then there was the human
inside of me that was the exact

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:39.271
opposite feeling.

00:05:39.304 --> 00:05:42.441
A sense of remorse.

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:47.146
A sense of, oh, we shouldn't
be celebrating anything.

00:05:47.179 --> 00:05:51.484
We're at the spot where
this amazing tragedy,

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:55.121
this amazing drama had unfolded.

00:05:56.488 --> 00:05:58.958
NARRATOR: Dr. Ballard's
discovery unleashed an explosion

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:02.928
of interest in the Titanic,
attracting tourists and treasure

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:05.297
hunters to the site.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.567
According to the law of the sea,
Ballard and his team could have

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.971
claimed the Titanic as their own
property by removing a single

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.106
artifact.

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:15.441
Bob Ballard: When we
found the Titanic,

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:18.043
it wouldn't have been that
difficult to go back and pick up

00:06:18.076 --> 00:06:21.013
something...but after
a lot of thought,

00:06:21.046 --> 00:06:25.317
we decided not to
disturb the site.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.788
NARRATOR: Two years later, RMS
Titanic Inc., a for-profit

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:35.027
company, mounted the first
of many salvage operations.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.431
The Titanic has even become
a tourist destination.

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:42.368
Tourist 1: It was very
sad, but very beautiful,

00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:44.537
and it was very
emotional for me.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.772
Tourist 2: It's something I've
wanted to do my whole life.

00:06:46.805 --> 00:06:49.442
Seen all the pictures, but
nothing compares to seeing

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:52.244
it when you're right there.

00:06:52.277 --> 00:06:54.146
NARRATOR: For the lucky
few who can afford

00:06:54.179 --> 00:06:58.451
the 60, 000-dollar fee, visitors
can board Russian subs and dive

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:01.487
to the shipwreck.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.691
With each visit, the
Titanic pays a price.

00:07:05.724 --> 00:07:07.026
Bob Ballard: Right there.

00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:09.862
See those big orange...that's
where the submarines have been

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:13.966
landing and you can see it's
just,it's breaking down the deck

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:16.135
It's crushing it.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:20.773
When you see orange, that's a
freshly exposed surface and

00:07:20.806 --> 00:07:22.408
that's not natural.

00:07:22.441 --> 00:07:25.177
That's being done by the people
coming and loving the thing

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.213
to death.

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:29.715
NARRATOR: With no international
agreements in place to protect

00:07:29.748 --> 00:07:35.287
the ship, Ballard sees
dramatic changes since 1985.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.990
Bob Ballard: The Titanic
is becoming a junk yard.

00:07:38.023 --> 00:07:41.894
You'll see big piles of chain
and big bags and, you know,

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:45.030
there's a lot of
trash down there.

00:07:45.063 --> 00:07:48.801
Up here, we're now going up
traversing the mast and when

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:51.270
we found it, it
had a crow's nest.

00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:52.905
NARRATOR: In
Ballard's first dives,

00:07:52.938 --> 00:07:56.408
he filmed the crow's nest where
lookouts spotted the iceberg

00:07:56.441 --> 00:07:59.812
that doomed the Titanic.

00:07:59.845 --> 00:08:04.250
Now, that crucial piece
of history...is gone,

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:08.053
apparently knocked into a cargo
hold by a passing submarine.

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:11.290
Bob Ballard: It's really sad
when I look at the damage that's

00:08:11.323 --> 00:08:14.326
been done by the submarines.

00:08:14.359 --> 00:08:17.396
We, in many ways,
opened Pandora's box.

00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:26.839
If the Titanic is not protected
and there's no guard on duty,

00:08:26.872 --> 00:08:28.641
it'll get stripped.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:31.310
It'll get stripped until all
the jewels have been taken off

00:08:31.343 --> 00:08:33.145
the old lady's body.

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:36.448
NARRATOR: On the 100th
anniversary of the Titanic

00:08:36.481 --> 00:08:41.186
disaster, Ballard is on
a quest to save the ship-before

00:08:41.219 --> 00:08:42.454
it's too late.

00:08:42.487 --> 00:08:44.490
Bob Ballard: So we're gonna
actually begin where it was

00:08:44.523 --> 00:08:47.593
built, up here in
northern Ireland.

00:08:47.626 --> 00:08:50.863
What I want to do is to
reconstruct the real story

00:08:50.896 --> 00:08:54.133
of the Titanic-from its
birthplace in Belfast,

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:58.871
to its final destination
in New York City.

00:08:58.904 --> 00:09:01.106
NARRATOR: And he wants to hear
directly from the families of

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:04.310
an elite team of Titanic heroes:

00:09:04.343 --> 00:09:08.781
Men who designed the
ship...built her with their own

00:09:08.814 --> 00:09:12.652
hands...and were selected to
sail on her maiden voyage.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.923
To track down their families,
Ballard will rely on an

00:09:17.956 --> 00:09:21.827
international network of
researchers...including a

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:25.864
Titanic buff who doesn't
collect artifacts

00:09:25.897 --> 00:09:28.434
he collects passengers.

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:31.837
Phil Gowan: I have all 2208
people that were on Titanic

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:33.772
listed in here.

00:09:33.805 --> 00:09:36.375
Their birth certificates, their
death certificates, their wills,

00:09:36.408 --> 00:09:39.946
their immigration
papers, passports.

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.082
NARRATOR: Phil Gowan's interest
in the Titanic began at

00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:44.917
an early age.

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:47.720
Phil Gowan: I can remember
very clearly my mother saying,

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:51.357
That boat sank 50
or 60 years ago.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.560
Why do you care about it now?

00:09:54.593 --> 00:09:58.831
I guess I wanted to know what
caused certain people to survive

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:02.168
and others to lose their lives.

00:10:03.235 --> 00:10:05.237
NARRATOR: Deep in Gowan's
research is information on

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:08.607
the men charged with making sure
the Titanic's maiden voyage went

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.609
smoothly.

00:10:10.642 --> 00:10:14.146
They were called
the guarantee group.

00:10:14.179 --> 00:10:17.516
Phil Gowan: And in that group
you had a master electrician,

00:10:17.549 --> 00:10:20.986
fitters, plumbers, draftsmen.

00:10:21.019 --> 00:10:23.856
They were checking to see that
everything that they had put

00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:26.125
together was
according to design.

00:10:29.594 --> 00:10:31.997
NARRATOR: Bob Ballard comes to
the city that these men called

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:37.102
home, where the Titanic
first rose to life: Belfast,

00:10:37.135 --> 00:10:38.838
northern Ireland.

00:10:40.605 --> 00:10:45.144
Bob Ballard: Well, the Titanic
story begins in Belfast.

00:10:45.177 --> 00:10:49.181
This was a community of
shipbuilders and built some

00:10:49.214 --> 00:10:51.083
of the greatest ships of all.

00:10:53.652 --> 00:10:56.388
NARRATOR: A century ago, this
dry dock was the largest on

00:10:56.421 --> 00:11:00.259
the planet, built for an epic
new class of ocean liner.

00:11:03.995 --> 00:11:06.765
Here tens of thousands
constructed the most massive

00:11:06.798 --> 00:11:10.903
moving objects ever built,
including a ship they knew as

00:11:10.936 --> 00:11:14.707
yard number 401.

00:11:16.742 --> 00:11:19.578
History remembers
her by another name.

00:11:20.579 --> 00:11:23.682
Bob Ballard: Everything
about the Titanic is titanic.

00:11:23.715 --> 00:11:25.250
There's nothing small.

00:11:25.283 --> 00:11:28.153
Everything is huge.

00:11:28.186 --> 00:11:30.022
Just look at this.

00:11:30.055 --> 00:11:34.626
This is where they installed
the massive propellers.

00:11:34.659 --> 00:11:37.262
They would have been right in
front of me, three of them,

00:11:37.295 --> 00:11:40.165
gigantic blades that
are now on the bottom

00:11:40.198 --> 00:11:41.600
of the North Atlantic.

00:11:44.803 --> 00:11:49.808
At the end of that dock,
I saw this wall of steel and

00:11:49.841 --> 00:11:53.779
it just reminded me of the
moment I saw the Titanic.

00:11:53.812 --> 00:11:56.015
And it was a flashback to
the moment of discovery.

00:11:59.785 --> 00:12:02.221
This was built from
the same steel,

00:12:02.254 --> 00:12:07.659
the same rivets-that's
the side of the Titanic.

00:12:07.692 --> 00:12:12.131
This is the first thing
I saw, the side of the ship.

00:12:12.164 --> 00:12:17.536
It was just monumental because
you can't see very far under

00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:19.304
the ocean.

00:12:19.337 --> 00:12:23.008
And so you're exploring this
vast part of our planet with

00:12:23.041 --> 00:12:25.010
a flashlight.

00:12:25.043 --> 00:12:27.346
And so you're shining your
flashlight and then all of

00:12:27.379 --> 00:12:32.017
a sudden something
comes into view.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:35.420
That really speaks to me.

00:12:35.453 --> 00:12:37.723
More so than anything.

00:12:39.825 --> 00:12:44.596
NARRATOR: Yet rivets and steel
are only part of the story.

00:12:44.629 --> 00:12:47.733
Getting to the heart of the
Titanic tragedy means finding

00:12:47.766 --> 00:12:51.670
the men who risked everything
to save Belfast's greatest

00:12:51.703 --> 00:12:52.471
creation.

00:13:00.478 --> 00:13:03.081
Bob Ballard has come to
northern Ireland to explore

00:13:03.114 --> 00:13:05.017
Titanic's past.

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:09.288
But his real concern is
the shipwreck's future.

00:13:09.321 --> 00:13:12.090
Bob Ballard: Ever since humans
have built great things like

00:13:12.123 --> 00:13:15.327
the pyramids of Giza or
the tombs of the Maya,

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.129
looters have been
there the next day.

00:13:19.397 --> 00:13:21.600
NARRATOR: Evidence presented
to a federal court suggests an

00:13:21.633 --> 00:13:27.539
unauthorized expedition to the
Titanic took place in 2002...

00:13:27.572 --> 00:13:33.045
and experts worry that more
rogue groups have plans for 2012

00:13:33.078 --> 00:13:35.614
Bob Ballard: Now that we're
moving away from using manned

00:13:35.647 --> 00:13:39.651
vehicles to ROVs, there are
thousands of them and they're

00:13:39.684 --> 00:13:41.620
in the hands of private people.

00:13:41.653 --> 00:13:44.189
So now the ball
game has changed.

00:13:44.222 --> 00:13:48.160
The Titanic's at greater
peril now than ever before.

00:13:48.193 --> 00:13:51.496
NARRATOR: If the Titanic is
plundered for her artifacts,

00:13:51.529 --> 00:13:55.267
the impact might be felt most
in her home town of Belfast.

00:13:58.904 --> 00:14:01.306
This was the home of
the guarantee group,

00:14:01.339 --> 00:14:04.176
a team of workers who built
the Titanic and would sail on

00:14:04.209 --> 00:14:06.445
her maiden voyage.

00:14:07.679 --> 00:14:09.882
Bob Ballard: For these men,
creating the Titanic was

00:14:09.915 --> 00:14:12.684
the pinnacle of a
decade's-long process.

00:14:12.717 --> 00:14:17.623
And it all happened here, at the
shipyards of Harland and Wolff.

00:14:17.656 --> 00:14:20.192
Bruce Beveridge: At one point
Harland and Wolff was the ship

00:14:20.225 --> 00:14:21.760
builder to the world.

00:14:21.793 --> 00:14:24.630
Now there was other companies
that were just as good.

00:14:24.663 --> 00:14:26.832
But Harland and Wolff was
probably known best of all for

00:14:26.865 --> 00:14:28.634
being an all around shipyard.

00:14:28.667 --> 00:14:31.403
In other words, the majority of
the fittings on their ships were

00:14:31.436 --> 00:14:34.239
built right on site.

00:14:34.272 --> 00:14:37.109
NARRATOR: Harland and Wolff had
an exclusive agreement to build

00:14:37.142 --> 00:14:41.380
ships for the White Star Line,
manufacturing almost every part

00:14:41.413 --> 00:14:45.617
of great ocean liners-from their
steel hulls to their elaborate

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:47.586
interiors.

00:14:47.619 --> 00:14:49.755
Bruce Beveridge: White Star Line
and Harland and Wolff were just

00:14:49.788 --> 00:14:51.590
incredible ship builders.

00:14:51.623 --> 00:14:56.228
Those two changed the way
people travel across the ocean.

00:14:56.261 --> 00:14:59.331
Every ship that you see now has
got inspiration from Harland

00:14:59.364 --> 00:15:02.301
and Wolff on it, whether
they know it or not.

00:15:04.269 --> 00:15:06.872
NARRATOR: One worker at Harland
and Wolff was a young man

00:15:06.905 --> 00:15:12.811
who would be forever linked to
the Titanic: Thomas Andrews.

00:15:12.844 --> 00:15:16.448
Phil Gowan: Thomas Andrews was
a well-connected young man.

00:15:16.481 --> 00:15:20.619
His uncle was Lord Pirrie,
who was in effect the owner

00:15:20.652 --> 00:15:23.222
of Harland and Wolff.

00:15:24.122 --> 00:15:26.124
Andrews was an
apprentice at first,

00:15:26.157 --> 00:15:29.761
and worked his way through
various departments and got

00:15:29.794 --> 00:15:33.198
a taste for the whole
shipbuilding industry.

00:15:33.231 --> 00:15:35.801
NARRATOR: Soon, Thomas
was a rising star,

00:15:35.834 --> 00:15:38.170
and his uncle put
him in charge of the design

00:15:38.203 --> 00:15:39.771
of new ocean liners

00:15:39.804 --> 00:15:41.573
Bruce Beveridge: It's
nepotism to the nith degree.

00:15:41.606 --> 00:15:45.210
However, in this instance, this
man was qualified for the job.

00:15:45.243 --> 00:15:48.280
Everybody loved Thomas Andrews,
they called him Tommy.

00:15:48.313 --> 00:15:49.948
He was a very personable man.

00:15:49.981 --> 00:15:51.450
He would walk around
in the shipyard,

00:15:51.483 --> 00:15:53.919
he would talk to the workers,
he would actually listen to them

00:15:53.952 --> 00:15:55.154
and try to do
something for them.

00:16:00.892 --> 00:16:03.161
NARRATOR: Andrews found himself
on the leading edge of a

00:16:03.194 --> 00:16:05.731
cutthroat industry.

00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:08.467
In an age when airplanes and
automobiles were in their

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:11.737
infancy, the steamship was king.

00:16:16.508 --> 00:16:19.277
Phil Gowan: In order to be the
most competitive business out

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:21.580
there in trans-Atlantic
shipping,

00:16:21.613 --> 00:16:24.583
you wanted to have the
state-of-the-art elevators,

00:16:24.616 --> 00:16:27.686
telephones had come into
the rooms in some of them.

00:16:27.719 --> 00:16:31.256
These were things that were
going to lure customers away

00:16:31.289 --> 00:16:32.624
from the competition.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.397
NARRATOR: The first decade of
the 20th century saw an ocean

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:44.369
liner arms race between the
White Star Line and Cunard.

00:16:44.402 --> 00:16:48.473
Cunard took a commanding lead
in 1907 with the Mauretania and

00:16:48.506 --> 00:16:53.045
Lusitania, the largest and
fastest ships on earth.

00:16:53.945 --> 00:16:57.249
White Star struck back with
ships 100 feet longer and

00:16:57.282 --> 00:17:02.721
unparalleled in luxury...
with elaborate smoking rooms,

00:17:02.754 --> 00:17:06.592
gymnasiums and heated
swimming pools.

00:17:07.926 --> 00:17:11.430
The first ship in this new
class would be called Olympic.

00:17:11.463 --> 00:17:13.766
The second, Titanic.

00:17:15.633 --> 00:17:16.902
Bruce Beveridge: With
the Olympic and Titanic,

00:17:16.935 --> 00:17:20.105
they really outdid Cunard and
they went way over the top.

00:17:20.138 --> 00:17:22.307
Their ships were so big that
they had a problem finding a

00:17:22.340 --> 00:17:25.277
harbor in order to dock them in
because nothing was deep enough.

00:17:27.212 --> 00:17:29.181
NARRATOR: Building these ships
in Belfast meant building

00:17:29.214 --> 00:17:33.452
a whole new infrastructure.

00:17:33.485 --> 00:17:37.222
Bob Ballard: To come here
is to feel the Titanic.

00:17:37.255 --> 00:17:40.058
Because you can see where it was
built, where it was outfitted,

00:17:40.091 --> 00:17:43.662
and there's a tremendous amount
associated with the building

00:17:43.695 --> 00:17:45.597
of the ship here.

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:47.632
NARRATOR: Like the offices
of the man who oversaw

00:17:47.665 --> 00:17:51.603
the construction of the
Titanic: Thomas Andrews.

00:17:51.636 --> 00:17:52.871
Bob Ballard: He's the architect.

00:17:52.904 --> 00:17:55.807
He's the most qualified person
and the most knowledgeable

00:17:55.840 --> 00:17:58.210
person about the Titanic.

00:17:58.243 --> 00:18:00.545
I have the greatest
respect for him.

00:18:00.578 --> 00:18:07.486
To actually be in his room in
Belfast at Harland and Wolff

00:18:07.519 --> 00:18:09.188
is pretty special.

00:18:10.722 --> 00:18:11.790
Pretty special.

00:18:11.823 --> 00:18:17.496
I can imagine him standing
here, fire burning,

00:18:17.529 --> 00:18:19.664
pondering the plans.

00:18:19.697 --> 00:18:24.369
Seeing something that's wrong
and correcting something.

00:18:24.402 --> 00:18:28.540
Probably had a pen right
here and made his notes,

00:18:28.573 --> 00:18:34.579
and rolled them up and
carried them across the hall.

00:18:34.612 --> 00:18:37.282
This was the place
where they built it.

00:18:37.315 --> 00:18:41.820
This is her, as far as I'm
concerned, her true home.

00:18:45.423 --> 00:18:47.592
NARRATOR: As the design and
construction of the Titanic got

00:18:47.625 --> 00:18:52.130
underway, thousands of Belfast
workers were also competing for

00:18:52.163 --> 00:18:56.668
a rare honor: the chance to join
Thomas Andrews on the ship's

00:18:56.701 --> 00:18:58.904
guarantee group.

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:00.705
Bruce Beveridge: They all had
their specialties, you know?

00:19:00.738 --> 00:19:03.008
They didn't just pick four guys
at Harland and Wolff and say,

00:19:03.041 --> 00:19:05.343
Hey, you want to go for a ride?

00:19:05.376 --> 00:19:08.547
NARRATOR: Only eight workers out
of more than ten thousand would

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:13.318
be chosen for the maiden voyage,
with the ninth, Thomas Andrews,

00:19:13.351 --> 00:19:14.920
as their leader.

00:19:14.953 --> 00:19:17.756
It was the honor of a lifetime.

00:19:17.789 --> 00:19:20.492
Bob Ballard: The guarantee
group were the experts;

00:19:20.525 --> 00:19:23.762
they knew the Titanic
better than anyone.

00:19:23.795 --> 00:19:26.798
The White Star Line and the
crew were just taking over the

00:19:26.831 --> 00:19:29.968
operation of the Titanic,
but the guarantee group were

00:19:30.001 --> 00:19:32.070
the ones that built it.

00:19:34.072 --> 00:19:36.141
NARRATOR: Bob Ballard has
tracked down the descendant

00:19:36.174 --> 00:19:38.944
of one guarantee group member.

00:19:38.977 --> 00:19:41.313
They've come to what was once
Harland and Wolff's design

00:19:41.346 --> 00:19:45.217
department, the drawing offices
where draftsmen planned out

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:48.787
the entire ship...by hand.

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:51.890
Deirdre: Roderick Chisholm, who
was chief draftsman and part

00:19:51.923 --> 00:19:55.927
of the guarantee group, was
my great-grandfather.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.064
Roderick was the right
hand man of Thomas Andrews.

00:19:59.097 --> 00:20:00.398
Bob Ballard: He was
number two, yeah.

00:20:00.431 --> 00:20:03.068
Deirdre: Yes, I was told that
nobody knew the ship better than

00:20:03.101 --> 00:20:05.337
Thomas Andrews and
Roderick Chisholm.

00:20:07.905 --> 00:20:11.109
NARRATOR: In this room, Andrews
and Chisholm oversaw the designs

00:20:11.142 --> 00:20:14.646
for Olympic and Titanic.

00:20:14.679 --> 00:20:17.148
Bruce Beveridge: I find it just
incredible that these men,

00:20:17.181 --> 00:20:20.485
without the use of
calculators or electronics,

00:20:20.518 --> 00:20:22.087
could build something like this.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:26.057
There's so many calculations
and there's so much work.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:28.560
You know, they didn't have a
tape measure that measured out

00:20:28.593 --> 00:20:31.663
882 feet, 9 inches.

00:20:31.696 --> 00:20:33.498
Deirdre: Harland and Wolff
didn't just build the ship;

00:20:33.531 --> 00:20:34.733
they fitted her out.

00:20:34.766 --> 00:20:38.403
So it just shows you what
we Belfast people are like.

00:20:38.436 --> 00:20:39.404
Bob Ballard: Yes.

00:20:39.437 --> 00:20:41.606
Deirdre: Say that
with great modesty.

00:20:41.639 --> 00:20:42.707
Bob Ballard: That was...

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:43.808
Deirdre: We were a
talented bunch in Belfast.

00:20:43.841 --> 00:20:45.143
Bob Ballard: ...quite
a ship you built.

00:20:45.176 --> 00:20:48.546
Deirdre: And I think, you know,
Titanic, it was a disaster,

00:20:48.579 --> 00:20:50.982
and it was a tragedy,
and it was awful.

00:20:51.015 --> 00:20:53.785
But she was magnificent.

00:20:53.818 --> 00:20:57.956
And she spoke of what
Belfast is, magnificent.

00:20:57.989 --> 00:21:01.192
NARRATOR: As chief draftsman
of this tremendous endeavor,

00:21:01.225 --> 00:21:03.828
Roderick Chisholm
was a natural choice to sail

00:21:03.861 --> 00:21:05.664
on the maiden voyage.

00:21:05.697 --> 00:21:07.699
Deirdre: How they
chose that group...

00:21:07.732 --> 00:21:09.434
Bob Ballard: They probably
took the best of the best....

00:21:09.467 --> 00:21:11.002
Deirdre: Well, I'm
assuming they did.

00:21:11.035 --> 00:21:13.038
But I mean, that would've
been an awesome honor and a,

00:21:13.071 --> 00:21:15.140
and a real privilege.

00:21:15.173 --> 00:21:16.041
Bob Ballard: To
be in that group.

00:21:16.074 --> 00:21:18.343
Deirdre: To be in that group.

00:21:18.376 --> 00:21:20.879
NARRATOR: Chisholm played
another key role-designing

00:21:20.912 --> 00:21:24.049
the Titanic's lifeboats.

00:21:24.749 --> 00:21:28.553
But he didn't have a say
in the number of lifeboats.

00:21:28.586 --> 00:21:32.490
Thomas Andrews
requested as many as 64.

00:21:32.523 --> 00:21:34.693
Bruce Beveridge: He wanted
more lifeboats in this ship.

00:21:34.726 --> 00:21:38.430
He made an argument for it and
of course he was shot down.

00:21:38.463 --> 00:21:40.465
NARRATOR: The heads of Harland
and Wolff and the White Star

00:21:40.498 --> 00:21:45.036
Line wanted more deck
space and less clutter.

00:21:45.069 --> 00:21:48.673
They insisted on no more than
20 lifeboats-four more than

00:21:48.706 --> 00:21:49.808
the legal minimum.

00:21:52.643 --> 00:21:56.348
It would be a fateful decision
for everyone on board.

00:22:00.885 --> 00:22:02.454
Auctioneer: Interested
in this lot?

00:22:02.487 --> 00:22:05.223
A black and white photograph
of Titanic's launch.

00:22:05.256 --> 00:22:09.327
Somewhere in the
region of six sterling.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:11.963
NARRATOR: Bidding is intense
at the auction in England,

00:22:11.996 --> 00:22:15.166
as collectors compete for
artifacts from Titanic's

00:22:15.199 --> 00:22:16.468
home town.

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:19.904
Auctioneer:At seven-thousand-six
at seven-thousand-six.

00:22:19.937 --> 00:22:23.208
Am I done? Yes, I am.

00:22:23.241 --> 00:22:25.377
Andrew Aldridge: What we've got
is a collection of material

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.712
relating to Belfast.

00:22:27.745 --> 00:22:29.781
Belfast was the
home of the Titanic.

00:22:29.814 --> 00:22:32.951
It was where Titanic was built,
it was where she spent all

00:22:32.984 --> 00:22:34.419
of her life.

00:22:34.452 --> 00:22:36.621
This is a young
man, Johnny Kelly.

00:22:36.654 --> 00:22:38.556
He was a worker at
Harland and Wolff.

00:22:38.589 --> 00:22:40.959
He was one of the first
men to die on Titanic.

00:22:40.992 --> 00:22:42.560
He died in June, 1910.

00:22:42.593 --> 00:22:46.898
He fell from the gantry, age 19,
almost two years before she sank

00:22:46.931 --> 00:22:48.366
beneath the North Atlantic.

00:22:48.399 --> 00:22:52.504
We have one of Titanic's rivets.
Never made it onto Titanic.

00:22:52.537 --> 00:22:55.340
One of over three
million from the ship.

00:22:55.373 --> 00:22:58.910
And finally, two letters
relating to the guarantee group,

00:22:58.943 --> 00:23:01.613
men who were on the Titanic,
employed by Harland and Wolff,

00:23:01.646 --> 00:23:05.083
to ensure that the elements
of the ship worked.

00:23:05.116 --> 00:23:07.218
If there was an
electrical issue,

00:23:07.251 --> 00:23:09.888
if there was a plumbing issue,
these specialists would be

00:23:09.921 --> 00:23:11.590
on hand to solve this problem.

00:23:13.558 --> 00:23:15.827
NARRATOR: Bob Ballard is piecing
together the story of the

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:19.731
guarantee group, with the help
of ancestry.com and the Belfast

00:23:19.764 --> 00:23:23.067
Titanic Society.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:26.137
He's come to the village of
Comber-ancestral home of the

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:29.141
group's leader, Thomas Andrews.

00:23:29.907 --> 00:23:32.444
Bob Ballard: I remember when I
was first thinking about trying

00:23:32.477 --> 00:23:36.481
to find her, and I thought
immediately of Thomas Andrews

00:23:36.514 --> 00:23:39.684
because that was the person
I, in all my research,

00:23:39.717 --> 00:23:41.686
I had the deepest respect for.

00:23:41.719 --> 00:23:43.721
Johnny: Yes, and we're now
celebrating that great

00:23:43.754 --> 00:23:44.956
achievement.

00:23:44.989 --> 00:23:49.060
Not just by one man-Uncle
Tommy-but by all his work mates.

00:23:49.093 --> 00:23:51.930
Bob Ballard: When you look at
the state of the technology

00:23:51.963 --> 00:23:57.235
and the shipbuilding, it was
really the cutting edge.

00:23:57.268 --> 00:23:59.637
Johnny: Yes, it was at the
pinnacle of technology,

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:01.339
at the peak of
the British empire.

00:24:01.372 --> 00:24:04.409
It was an exciting time and
Belfast was at the forefront

00:24:04.442 --> 00:24:05.043
of all that.

00:24:07.512 --> 00:24:09.881
NARRATOR: But some have said
there's a fatal flaw in Thomas

00:24:09.914 --> 00:24:13.151
Andrews' design of the Titanic.

00:24:13.184 --> 00:24:17.222
To find out, Ballard has come
to a conservation lab outside

00:24:17.255 --> 00:24:21.493
Belfast to examine the
original ship plans.

00:24:21.526 --> 00:24:23.895
NARRATOR: They've never
been filmed before.

00:24:23.928 --> 00:24:26.364
William Blair: We are fortunate
to be the repository for

00:24:26.397 --> 00:24:28.933
the Harland and Wolff
ship plan collection,

00:24:28.966 --> 00:24:33.238
which includes the original
designs for Olympic and Titanic.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:39.143
Bob Ballard: Well, this is a
very familiar drawing to me.

00:24:39.176 --> 00:24:44.148
I see the forward mast and the
crow's nest and the ship's bell.

00:24:44.181 --> 00:24:47.218
This drawing here
of the poop deck,

00:24:47.251 --> 00:24:53.691
the after section of the Titanic
really for me has such symbolism

00:24:53.724 --> 00:24:57.362
because as the bow went down,
people just kept progressing

00:24:57.395 --> 00:25:00.999
backwards and
backwards to the stern.

00:25:01.032 --> 00:25:03.668
Then finally they came to
where there was nowhere to go.

00:25:03.701 --> 00:25:05.803
They just jumped.

00:25:05.836 --> 00:25:08.640
Jumping in most
cases to their death.

00:25:10.975 --> 00:25:12.243
NARRATOR: One
hundred years later,

00:25:12.276 --> 00:25:15.413
experts still want to know if
the Titanic was a ticking time

00:25:15.446 --> 00:25:20.251
bomb, her fate written
into her plans.

00:25:20.284 --> 00:25:23.421
The answer might lie in
Titanic's twin sister,

00:25:23.454 --> 00:25:27.358
built from the same set
of drawings: the Olympic.

00:25:27.391 --> 00:25:29.227
Bruce Beveridge: This has caused
a lot of confusion over time and

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:32.096
as you can see from the profiles
here-this is the Olympic

00:25:32.129 --> 00:25:33.498
and this is the Titanic.

00:25:33.531 --> 00:25:35.199
Unless you knew what
you were looking at,

00:25:35.232 --> 00:25:37.936
you would confuse
one for the other.

00:25:37.969 --> 00:25:40.271
NARRATOR: While almost
identical on paper,

00:25:40.304 --> 00:25:44.776
Titanic and Olympic had
strikingly different careers.

00:25:44.809 --> 00:25:47.278
William Blair: Olympic really
had the career in a sense

00:25:47.311 --> 00:25:48.479
that Titanic would have had...

00:25:48.512 --> 00:25:49.547
Bob Ballard: Yeah, it had
hundreds of crossings.

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:51.983
William Blair: ...had it
not met its tragic fate.

00:25:53.985 --> 00:25:55.787
NARRATOR: The Olympic was
known as old reliable,

00:25:55.820 --> 00:26:00.625
with over 20 years of
distinguished service.

00:26:00.658 --> 00:26:03.962
Built from the same plans,
the Titanic should have lasted

00:26:03.995 --> 00:26:06.764
at least as long.

00:26:06.797 --> 00:26:09.367
Bruce Beveridge: There were
no design flaws in that ship.

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:12.971
That ship was built to withstand
any of the stresses and strains

00:26:13.004 --> 00:26:17.141
that are brought on, even by the
heaviest North Atlantic gales

00:26:17.174 --> 00:26:21.479
It was not designed
to sideswipe icebergs.

00:26:22.413 --> 00:26:24.482
William Blair: As they
used to say in Belfast,

00:26:24.515 --> 00:26:28.319
she was all right
when she left us.

00:26:28.352 --> 00:26:31.155
NARRATOR: And she
was in good hands.

00:26:31.188 --> 00:26:34.225
As the Titanic rose above
the Belfast skyline,

00:26:34.258 --> 00:26:37.528
Thomas Andrews was joined on the
guarantee group by the best and

00:26:37.561 --> 00:26:40.331
brightest at Harland and Wolff:

00:26:40.364 --> 00:26:43.801
Among them, chief draftsman,
Roderick Chisholm...

00:26:43.834 --> 00:26:46.337
...electrician, William Parr...

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:49.107
...and Artie Frost,
a master fitter,

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:53.177
whose family still keeps
the tools of his trade.

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:57.415
Ian Frost: I have an item here
that belonged to my grandfather.

00:26:57.448 --> 00:27:02.954
And this whistle was a
most useful thing to have,

00:27:02.987 --> 00:27:05.490
because when you were down at
Harland and Wolff's in those

00:27:05.523 --> 00:27:08.827
days, riveting was a
very, very noisy job.

00:27:11.729 --> 00:27:15.533
If you wanted to draw somebody's
attention to the fact you wanted

00:27:15.566 --> 00:27:18.703
to speak to them, you used the
whistle because they wouldn't

00:27:18.736 --> 00:27:21.072
a heard you if you shouted.

00:27:24.308 --> 00:27:26.378
Still works, can I use it?...

00:27:31.782 --> 00:27:34.886
NARRATOR: In 1912, Artie Frost
got word that he'd been chosen

00:27:34.919 --> 00:27:38.489
to represent his department
on the guarantee group.

00:27:38.522 --> 00:27:41.192
Ian Frost: Not everybody got
sent in the guarantee group and

00:27:41.225 --> 00:27:44.829
an awful lot of the employees
would have loved to have gone.

00:27:46.831 --> 00:27:48.599
NARRATOR: Rounding out this
distinguished group was

00:27:48.632 --> 00:27:52.136
engineer, Robert Knight, and
four up and coming apprentices,

00:27:52.169 --> 00:27:56.908
including a 21-year-old
named Francis Parkes.

00:27:56.941 --> 00:27:58.576
Fred Parkes: My uncle,
he was a plumber.

00:27:58.609 --> 00:28:01.479
And he was 21 when he
was on the Titanic,

00:28:01.512 --> 00:28:04.449
and he came out as
a top apprentice.

00:28:04.482 --> 00:28:06.751
And he was selected
to go on this group...

00:28:06.784 --> 00:28:08.019
Bob Ballard: So it
was a great honor.

00:28:08.052 --> 00:28:10.188
Fred Parkes: A great honor
to go on this, to be on this,

00:28:10.221 --> 00:28:11.456
this ship.

00:28:11.489 --> 00:28:12.724
Bob Ballard: And so, when
did you first-I mean,

00:28:12.757 --> 00:28:15.560
as soon as you were born, I
imagine, or can remember,

00:28:15.593 --> 00:28:18.062
it must have entered your life,
the story of the Titanic.

00:28:18.095 --> 00:28:19.997
Fred Parkes: Oh, well, it
was something that you-it was

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:21.532
a tragedy that you
didn't talk about.

00:28:21.565 --> 00:28:22.734
Bob Ballard: Right. Well,
was it an embarrassment?

00:28:22.767 --> 00:28:24.002
Fred Parkes: It was an
embarrassment, yes.

00:28:25.536 --> 00:28:27.638
Bob Ballard: The sinking of
the Titanic was a tremendous

00:28:27.671 --> 00:28:32.043
embarrassment to the city of
Belfast, to Harland and Wolff,

00:28:32.076 --> 00:28:34.879
to the very heart of this
community and was not something

00:28:34.912 --> 00:28:37.015
they wanted to talk about.

00:28:38.115 --> 00:28:40.251
NARRATOR: Yet when
Titanic was ready to sail,

00:28:40.284 --> 00:28:42.253
she was the toast of Belfast.

00:28:44.421 --> 00:28:47.325
Thomas Andrews and the rest of
the guarantee group boarded the

00:28:47.358 --> 00:28:52.063
ship on April 2nd, 1912, bound
for Southampton and ultimately

00:28:52.096 --> 00:28:53.498
New York.

00:28:54.832 --> 00:28:56.901
Phil Gowan: I can only guess
what Thomas Andrews and the

00:28:56.934 --> 00:29:00.772
guarantee group might have felt
when they first stepped aboard.

00:29:00.805 --> 00:29:04.509
But satisfaction is
what it had to be,

00:29:04.542 --> 00:29:06.911
because nothing had ever
been like this before.

00:29:06.944 --> 00:29:12.016
And the fact that it had been
conceived, drafted, constructed,

00:29:12.049 --> 00:29:14.352
and now it was a
finished product,

00:29:14.385 --> 00:29:18.490
had to be the ultimate climax
for their lives at that point

00:29:24.962 --> 00:29:26.998
NARRATOR: The Titanic took
on passengers and crew at

00:29:27.031 --> 00:29:30.568
Southampton, eight days
after leaving its birthplace

00:29:30.601 --> 00:29:32.737
in Belfast.

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:36.240
As the journey began, the
guarantee group set to work

00:29:36.273 --> 00:29:40.178
inspecting all
aspects of the ship,

00:29:40.211 --> 00:29:43.481
from the engine
room to the bridge,

00:29:43.514 --> 00:29:48.453
and from the four kitchens to
the luxurious dining rooms.

00:29:52.089 --> 00:29:55.560
Until the fifth night at sea...

00:29:55.593 --> 00:30:03.201
April 14th, 1912: Titanic
steaming at 22 knots...a quiet

00:30:03.234 --> 00:30:07.772
Sunday night interrupted by the
sound of an iceberg tearing into

00:30:07.805 --> 00:30:09.574
the ship.

00:30:11.909 --> 00:30:15.980
At this critical moment, Captain
Edward Smith turns to the man

00:30:16.013 --> 00:30:18.549
who knows the Titanic best.

00:30:18.582 --> 00:30:20.284
Bob Ballard: When
they hit the iceberg,

00:30:20.317 --> 00:30:25.056
Captain Smith summoned
Thomas Andrews.

00:30:25.089 --> 00:30:28.693
The captain asked him to
go below decks and assess

00:30:28.726 --> 00:30:32.263
the damage to the ship
which he knew so well.

00:30:32.296 --> 00:30:36.901
And he went below decks and
walked the compartments and

00:30:36.934 --> 00:30:44.876
returned to the bridge and told
the captain it was gonna sink.

00:30:44.909 --> 00:30:48.880
NARRATOR: Despite being designed
with 16 watertight compartments,

00:30:48.913 --> 00:30:53.618
Andrews knew that no
ship is truly unsinkable.

00:30:53.651 --> 00:30:55.353
Bruce Beveridge: Titanic was
built to a two-compartment

00:30:55.386 --> 00:30:56.721
standard.

00:30:56.754 --> 00:30:58.756
We could have any of these two
compartments flooded-whether it

00:30:58.789 --> 00:31:02.360
be this one and this one,
or this one and this one-and

00:31:02.393 --> 00:31:05.329
she would be able to sail back
to port without a problem.

00:31:05.362 --> 00:31:08.266
You could even have the first
four watertight compartments

00:31:08.299 --> 00:31:10.968
flooded at the same time
and it wouldn't be an issue.

00:31:11.001 --> 00:31:16.674
However, you can't damage one,
two, three, four and five.

00:31:16.707 --> 00:31:18.976
That was a death
knell to the ship.

00:31:19.009 --> 00:31:20.845
What happens is there
is too much weight.

00:31:20.878 --> 00:31:23.014
And if you have too many
watertight compartments flooded,

00:31:23.047 --> 00:31:24.082
the ship's gonna sink.

00:31:27.518 --> 00:31:30.221
NARRATOR: Soon after Andrews
broke the news to the captain,

00:31:30.254 --> 00:31:35.960
the outside world got its first
clue that Titanic was in danger.

00:31:35.993 --> 00:31:38.362
Phil Gowan: You had the wireless
for the first time on some of

00:31:38.395 --> 00:31:39.463
these ships.

00:31:39.496 --> 00:31:44.135
And they began to get word
that something was up.

00:31:44.168 --> 00:31:46.170
NARRATOR: Distress calls
emanated from the greatest

00:31:46.203 --> 00:31:47.872
ship on the ocean.

00:31:47.905 --> 00:31:49.907
Phil Gowan: People couldn't
believe their ears.

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:52.777
They thought no, this
has gotta be a mistake.

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:56.380
Surely that ship couldn't sink.

00:31:56.413 --> 00:31:57.848
NARRATOR: In
Titanic's home town,

00:31:57.881 --> 00:32:02.687
families of the ship's guarantee
group waited anxiously for word.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:05.623
Ian Frost: Rumors in Belfast
were rife at the time.

00:32:05.656 --> 00:32:09.427
And my grandmother must have
been a very worried person

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:13.164
because she had heard
the Titanic had sank.

00:32:13.197 --> 00:32:17.501
She had also heard the Titanic
had been towed into port without

00:32:17.534 --> 00:32:18.836
loss of life.

00:32:18.869 --> 00:32:23.574
And she didn't know for three
or four days whether her husband

00:32:23.607 --> 00:32:25.977
was alive or dead.

00:32:28.278 --> 00:32:31.515
NARRATOR: The news
came first to New York.

00:32:31.548 --> 00:32:35.052
Bob Ballard: This was Titanic's
destination-the goal of hundreds

00:32:35.085 --> 00:32:39.156
of immigrants on board, and the
home of many of the ship's rich

00:32:39.189 --> 00:32:41.993
and famous.

00:32:42.026 --> 00:32:44.428
NARRATOR: The White Star Line
offices at the southern tip of

00:32:44.461 --> 00:32:50.368
Manhattan now became the focal
point of the Titanic drama.

00:32:50.401 --> 00:32:52.870
Bob Ballard: Well, right behind
me is Bowling Green Park at

00:32:52.903 --> 00:32:54.638
the tip of Manhattan Island.

00:32:54.671 --> 00:32:57.608
Right across the street here
at the end of Broadway is where

00:32:57.641 --> 00:33:00.411
the White Star Line had
their headquarters.

00:33:00.444 --> 00:33:02.446
This is where the reports
started coming in that

00:33:02.479 --> 00:33:06.450
the Titanic had hit an
iceberg on April 14th.

00:33:06.483 --> 00:33:11.155
But by late on the 15th, the
true story began to unfold.

00:33:12.256 --> 00:33:14.692
NARRATOR: An ocean liner called
the Carpathia had come to the

00:33:14.725 --> 00:33:18.562
Titanic's rescue, but they
arrived too late to the save

00:33:18.595 --> 00:33:20.097
the ship.

00:33:20.130 --> 00:33:23.267
Instead, they picked up
lifeboats filled with survivors,

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:27.805
and sent lists of names
to New York by radio.

00:33:27.838 --> 00:33:30.374
Bob Ballard: They started
posting the names of the

00:33:30.407 --> 00:33:34.712
survivors and the family members
came to find out if their

00:33:34.745 --> 00:33:39.017
relatives and friends lived
or died on the Titanic.

00:33:41.785 --> 00:33:44.522
Imagine the trickle of people
that began to assemble in this

00:33:44.555 --> 00:33:47.124
park and then it just
got bigger and bigger.

00:33:47.157 --> 00:33:50.928
Hundreds and hundreds of people
assembling here directly across

00:33:50.961 --> 00:33:54.765
the street wondering.

00:33:54.798 --> 00:33:57.401
Some went up, read
the good news,

00:33:57.434 --> 00:34:03.107
a lot never saw the name they
hoped for and went away in tears

00:34:09.346 --> 00:34:11.582
NARRATOR: The scene repeated
itself on both sides of

00:34:11.615 --> 00:34:14.085
the Atlantic.

00:34:14.118 --> 00:34:16.587
As each new name
went on the list,

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:20.257
a family would breathe
a sigh of relief.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:25.096
Yet days later, there was still
no word on the nine men of

00:34:25.129 --> 00:34:26.998
the guarantee group.

00:34:32.503 --> 00:34:35.873
The rescue ship Carpathia
arrived in New York on the night

00:34:35.906 --> 00:34:41.145
of April 18th, 1912, packed with
hundreds of Titanic survivors.

00:34:41.178 --> 00:34:42.880
Phil Gowan: It was a mob scene.

00:34:42.913 --> 00:34:45.950
There were people's names that
were included as having died

00:34:45.983 --> 00:34:48.519
that actually had
survived and vice versa.

00:34:48.552 --> 00:34:52.022
So people discovered for the
first time that night what it

00:34:52.055 --> 00:34:54.558
really happened.

00:34:54.591 --> 00:34:56.927
NARRATOR: As survivors
told their stories,

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:01.031
they began to shed light on the
leader of the guarantee group:

00:35:01.064 --> 00:35:02.300
Thomas Andrews.

00:35:04.835 --> 00:35:06.670
Bruce Beveridge: Thomas
Andrews was, you know,

00:35:06.703 --> 00:35:09.474
trying to assist in getting
the life jackets out.

00:35:12.009 --> 00:35:14.178
There is testimony that, you
know, that he told one woman,

00:35:14.211 --> 00:35:19.150
you know, here's your life
jacket, take it and go.

00:35:19.183 --> 00:35:22.153
NARRATOR: He didn't
heed his own advice.

00:35:22.186 --> 00:35:24.388
Phil Gowan: Thomas Andrews was
seen in the first class smoking

00:35:24.421 --> 00:35:26.457
room towards the end.

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:30.194
He was standing beside a big
painting that was above

00:35:30.227 --> 00:35:34.231
the fireplace, and he was
staring out into the distance.

00:35:34.264 --> 00:35:35.599
Bob Ballard: He
gave up his life.

00:35:35.632 --> 00:35:38.002
He didn't even bother
to put on a life jacket.

00:35:38.035 --> 00:35:40.804
He didn't even bother to
go up to the boat deck.

00:35:40.837 --> 00:35:45.610
He was last seen admiring his
ship and went down with her.

00:35:47.644 --> 00:35:50.014
NARRATOR: And the rest
of the guarantee group?

00:35:50.047 --> 00:35:52.750
Artie Frost and William Parr
were last seen in the ship's

00:35:52.783 --> 00:35:56.620
engine room, likely fighting
to keep the lights on so more

00:35:56.653 --> 00:35:59.557
passengers could escape.

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:02.393
Bob Ballard: The guarantee group
I'm sure were very involved

00:36:02.426 --> 00:36:04.295
in the final moments
of the Titanic,

00:36:04.328 --> 00:36:07.565
because they were the ones
that truly understood what had

00:36:07.598 --> 00:36:09.667
happened to the ship and
were trying to do their best

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:11.268
to save her.

00:36:11.301 --> 00:36:13.938
NARRATOR: They were
never seen again.

00:36:13.971 --> 00:36:16.840
Bruce Beveridge: None of the
guarantee group survived.

00:36:16.873 --> 00:36:17.975
None of them.

00:36:20.911 --> 00:36:23.414
Phil Gowan: The fact that none
of their bodies were ever found,

00:36:23.447 --> 00:36:25.349
that there really
aren't accounts,

00:36:25.382 --> 00:36:28.385
indicates that they didn't
really make any attempt to get

00:36:28.418 --> 00:36:31.522
into lifeboats or
save themselves.

00:36:31.555 --> 00:36:33.724
It doesn't appear that
any of them even tried.

00:36:36.994 --> 00:36:40.097
Ian Frost: This is the postcard
my grandfather Artie Frost sent

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:44.401
to my father, Stanley, just
before he got on the Titanic

00:36:44.434 --> 00:36:48.472
at Southampton and sailed away.

00:36:48.505 --> 00:36:53.611
It says, How would you like
to be fishing off this pier?

00:36:53.644 --> 00:36:56.614
Don't be having any more falls.

00:36:56.647 --> 00:36:59.617
My father had just fallen off
his bike and taken the knee off

00:36:59.650 --> 00:37:03.554
himself and he was hobbling
around at that time.

00:37:03.587 --> 00:37:06.790
And that's the picture of
the pier at Southampton where

00:37:06.823 --> 00:37:10.194
the Titanic was tied up.

00:37:10.227 --> 00:37:15.099
This was the last word the
family had from him, this card.

00:37:15.132 --> 00:37:18.035
And they never heard
from him again.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:23.407
And that does something
to me when I say that.

00:37:27.411 --> 00:37:31.882
NARRATOR: Of the 2,200 people
on Titanic's maiden voyage,

00:37:31.915 --> 00:37:34.618
only 700 survived.

00:37:34.651 --> 00:37:38.922
More than 1,500 died.

00:37:38.955 --> 00:37:43.060
As early as 1912, families of
the victims proposed expeditions

00:37:43.093 --> 00:37:47.564
to locate the wreckage.

00:37:47.597 --> 00:37:50.601
Yet even as deep-sea
technology improved,

00:37:50.634 --> 00:37:55.106
the Titanic remained
beyond reach for 73 years.

00:37:57.040 --> 00:38:00.477
For Bob Ballard, it took a clue
from a Titanic survivor to show

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:01.612
him the way.

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:07.084
At the pier in New York,
where survivors came ashore,

00:38:07.117 --> 00:38:10.788
Ballard meets the
grandson of Jack Thayer.

00:38:10.821 --> 00:38:13.824
Jack was one of the last people
to jump from the stern of

00:38:13.857 --> 00:38:17.061
the Titanic and live.

00:38:17.094 --> 00:38:18.762
Ed Thayer: He and a friend
jumped from the same rail at

00:38:18.795 --> 00:38:19.730
the same time.

00:38:19.763 --> 00:38:21.799
His friend didn't
survive, he did.

00:38:21.832 --> 00:38:26.870
So, it was just
probably by sheer luck.

00:38:26.903 --> 00:38:30.274
And by finding an overturned
lifeboat and having some place

00:38:30.307 --> 00:38:32.876
to sort of call
home for the night,

00:38:32.909 --> 00:38:37.014
he was able to get out
of the water directly.

00:38:37.047 --> 00:38:39.883
NARRATOR: Jack's account was one
of the first to say the Titanic

00:38:39.916 --> 00:38:43.654
broke in half as it sank.

00:38:43.687 --> 00:38:46.857
Bob Ballard: He said, The rumble
and the roar continued with even

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:51.061
louder distinct wrenching and
tearing of boilers and engines

00:38:51.094 --> 00:38:52.396
from their beds.

00:38:52.429 --> 00:38:55.699
Suddenly, the whole super
structure of the ship appeared

00:38:55.732 --> 00:38:57.301
to split...

00:38:57.334 --> 00:39:02.673
That's what led me to realize
that the ship broke in half

00:39:02.706 --> 00:39:04.175
and dumped out its contents.

00:39:07.177 --> 00:39:11.548
The story he told gave me
an idea on how to find her,

00:39:11.581 --> 00:39:14.551
and that was not to
look for the Titanic,

00:39:14.584 --> 00:39:16.987
but I would look for the debris
that came off the Titanic.

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:20.057
Because I knew its footprint
would be much larger

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:21.492
and predictable.

00:39:21.525 --> 00:39:24.461
We knew the current so we knew
the debris field should be

00:39:24.494 --> 00:39:25.596
north-south.

00:39:25.629 --> 00:39:28.799
That's huge. 'Cause now
I have a line this way.

00:39:28.832 --> 00:39:31.068
I search this way.

00:39:31.101 --> 00:39:33.971
And sure enough, I picked up
the footprints and it led me

00:39:34.004 --> 00:39:36.507
to the Titanic.

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:39.109
NARRATOR: But now that Ballard
has found the Titanic,

00:39:39.142 --> 00:39:44.081
he faces a far more daunting
task: finding a way to protect

00:39:44.114 --> 00:39:48.552
it and its thousands of
priceless artifacts.

00:39:54.691 --> 00:40:02.032
Nikki: Got my menus! And a menu
holder. So that's good. Happy.

00:40:02.065 --> 00:40:04.268
NARRATOR: As the Titanic
auction concludes,

00:40:04.301 --> 00:40:08.639
the net sales add up to
over 300,000 dollars.

00:40:08.672 --> 00:40:11.108
Andrew Aldridge: We had a really
motivated crowd here today,

00:40:11.141 --> 00:40:14.445
very enthusiastic collectors
who're vying to buy some very

00:40:14.478 --> 00:40:15.579
interesting pieces.

00:40:15.612 --> 00:40:18.182
And we were very pleased
with how today went.

00:40:18.215 --> 00:40:20.717
NARRATOR: The keys from the
Titanic sell for more than

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:24.054
55,000 dollars.

00:40:24.087 --> 00:40:26.723
NARRATOR: Two guarantee group
artifacts go for more than

00:40:26.756 --> 00:40:28.725
700 dollars each.

00:40:28.758 --> 00:40:30.561
NARRATOR: They are letters of
condolence to the grieving

00:40:30.594 --> 00:40:33.931
families.

00:40:33.964 --> 00:40:37.868
Some of the items in the
auction are bound for museums.

00:40:37.901 --> 00:40:42.473
Others will disappear
from public view.

00:40:42.506 --> 00:40:45.576
As more and more items
fall into private hands,

00:40:45.609 --> 00:40:49.646
there remains a key storehouse
for Titanic artifacts:

00:40:49.679 --> 00:40:53.050
the ship itself.

00:40:53.083 --> 00:40:55.919
And it's only getting easier
to reach what was once

00:40:55.952 --> 00:40:59.256
an inaccessible depth.

00:40:59.289 --> 00:41:00.757
David Gallo: Much of the
technology we use today to

00:41:00.790 --> 00:41:04.628
explore in the ocean, those are
the grandchildren of the tools

00:41:04.661 --> 00:41:06.764
we used on Titanic.

00:41:08.698 --> 00:41:11.602
NARRATOR: This technology used
to be the exclusive domain

00:41:11.635 --> 00:41:16.507
of elite oceanographers...
but those days are numbered.

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:17.908
David Gallo: Today, if
you've got the money,

00:41:17.941 --> 00:41:21.478
it's very easy to go out and
find a ship, to find a robot,

00:41:21.511 --> 00:41:23.680
get the cables, get a crew
together to go look at some

00:41:23.713 --> 00:41:26.016
place like Titanic.

00:41:26.049 --> 00:41:28.318
Let's face it, Titanic's
1,000 miles from New York,

00:41:28.351 --> 00:41:30.287
it's 400 miles
from Newfoundland,

00:41:30.320 --> 00:41:34.758
pretty hard to enforce
that site so far away.

00:41:34.791 --> 00:41:37.194
Bob Ballard: There's
no international laws.

00:41:37.227 --> 00:41:41.832
Any rogue organization
could go out there.

00:41:41.865 --> 00:41:47.204
I'm mostly concerned about the
technology now to get inside.

00:41:47.237 --> 00:41:50.741
And that's where the
preservation's the highest,

00:41:50.774 --> 00:41:53.878
and most of the
contents are there.

00:41:57.981 --> 00:41:59.550
NARRATOR: After
decades of waiting,

00:41:59.583 --> 00:42:01.318
an international treaty and U.S.

00:42:01.351 --> 00:42:05.222
legislation are now on the
table-and could start protecting

00:42:05.255 --> 00:42:08.959
the ship as early as 2012.

00:42:08.992 --> 00:42:12.129
Bob Ballard believes the
same ROVs that make it easier

00:42:12.162 --> 00:42:17.167
to reach the Titanic, could
also make it easier to police.

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:20.871
Bob Ballard: Do I expect others
to go after artifacts from

00:42:20.904 --> 00:42:23.774
the Titanic, covertly
or quietly? Yes.

00:42:23.807 --> 00:42:26.944
Now, is there technology
that we could build sentries?

00:42:26.977 --> 00:42:27.811
Absolutely.

00:42:27.844 --> 00:42:31.682
Could we have guards?
Yes, easily.

00:42:31.715 --> 00:42:35.018
NARRATOR: In fact, a pilot
project in the Pacific will

00:42:35.051 --> 00:42:38.855
allow robots to constantly
monitor the ocean floor-a system

00:42:38.888 --> 00:42:42.025
that could someday provide
24-hour security for

00:42:42.058 --> 00:42:44.895
the Titanic.

00:42:44.928 --> 00:42:48.632
But to Ballard, any solution
starts with the people most

00:42:48.665 --> 00:42:51.168
affected by the tragedy.

00:42:51.201 --> 00:42:53.704
Bob Ballard: How would you like
the site treated in the future?

00:42:53.737 --> 00:42:56.206
Fred Parkes: I would like
it to be left as it is.

00:42:56.239 --> 00:43:00.777
That's my uncle's graveyard.
And you don't desecrate a grave

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:02.512
Deirdre: I know my
great-grandfather, Roderick,

00:43:02.545 --> 00:43:06.183
he's down there.
That's his grave.

00:43:06.216 --> 00:43:09.453
And I know my grandmother was
extremely adamant that it should

00:43:09.486 --> 00:43:12.089
not be touched, the graves
of all of them shouldn't be

00:43:12.122 --> 00:43:14.124
tampered with...

00:43:14.157 --> 00:43:17.394
Bob Ballard: Every person
I've spoken to that has any

00:43:17.427 --> 00:43:20.831
relationship to the
Titanic, have all said,

00:43:20.864 --> 00:43:23.200
Please protect this ship.

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:25.002
Johnny: And, you know, my
grandfather remembered being at

00:43:25.035 --> 00:43:28.271
the launch and I can still hear
his booming voice saying it

00:43:28.304 --> 00:43:30.440
should be left to rest in peace.

00:43:30.473 --> 00:43:31.642
Bob Ballard: That's my hope.

00:43:31.675 --> 00:43:35.379
Certainly all the survivors,
when I first found her,

00:43:35.412 --> 00:43:37.581
there were several survivors
that were still alive,

00:43:37.614 --> 00:43:39.449
and they all felt the same way.

00:43:39.482 --> 00:43:42.552
Johnny: It's really important
that it's left as a memorial,

00:43:42.585 --> 00:43:49.393
as a monument to the great
work that went on in Belfast.

00:43:49.426 --> 00:43:53.397
NARRATOR: In downtown Belfast,
a monument stands in honor

00:43:53.430 --> 00:44:01.805
of 22 local men who lost their
lives on April 15th, 1912.

00:44:01.838 --> 00:44:06.910
Nine of them belong to
the guarantee group.

00:44:06.943 --> 00:44:11.415
Bob Ballard: I didn't really
get touched by the Titanic until

00:44:11.448 --> 00:44:13.383
I found it.

00:44:13.416 --> 00:44:16.153
You know, I'm a scientist and
I looked at it as a technical

00:44:16.186 --> 00:44:19.890
thing and, but when
we found it, that was,

00:44:19.923 --> 00:44:23.460
it really hit me
and I was surprised.

00:44:26.496 --> 00:44:28.031
NARRATOR: For the
guarantee group,

00:44:28.064 --> 00:44:32.269
and for over a thousand others
who died on the Titanic,

00:44:32.302 --> 00:44:36.106
this ship is their grave.

00:44:36.139 --> 00:44:43.881
Every inch is sacred ground.
Every artifact...a memorial.

00:44:46.916 --> 00:44:48.185
Bob Ballard: You know,
my feeling is that if

00:44:48.218 --> 00:44:52.422
you can't protect the Titanic,
there is nothing in the sea

00:44:52.455 --> 00:44:54.357
you can protect.

00:44:54.390 --> 00:44:58.662
So, that's why I want
to stand my ground.

00:44:58.695 --> 00:45:03.667
We're not done until
it's protected.

